# `send: exit(...)` in the root of a program (the book, send): the exit code of the letter is an int expression made of a
# local and a call, 3 - 2 = 1. The process ends with that code, so the row pins the exit 1 (a check of the graph rows
# compares the dump after the run only for the exit 0) and every other code of the expression is a failure of the row.
fn: two () int
    return: 2
int: base 3
send: exit(exit_code: base - two(); stdout: ""; stderr: "")
return
